Latticra Licensing
==================

Latticra uses a transition-aware hybrid open licensing model.

Summary
-------

```text
Core/runtime/security substrate: AGPL-3.0-or-later
SDKs, examples, packaging helpers, installer glue, and integration helpers: Apache-2.0
Documentation, handbooks, architecture notes, and policy notes: CC-BY-4.0
Latticra/Bryforge names, logos, marks, and identity: TRADEMARK_POLICY.md
```

Core runtime, security-substrate, tool-boundary, runtime-gating,
verification, and evidence-bound software work is licensed, or intended
for new work to be licensed, under:

    AGPL-3.0-or-later

SDKs, examples, packaging helpers, installer glue, integration helpers,
and other adoption-facing software are licensed under:

    Apache-2.0

Documentation, handbooks, architecture notes, policy notes, and public
reader-facing project material are licensed under:

    CC-BY-4.0

The Latticra and Bryforge names, logos, marks, slogans, and distinctive
identity are not open-licensed by default. They are governed separately
by:

    TRADEMARK_POLICY.md

Transition Rule
---------------

Existing repository code remains Apache-2.0 unless a file-level SPDX
identifier, path-level notice, or later reviewed migration explicitly
marks it otherwise.

New core software work should use:

    SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later

New adoption-facing helper code should use:

    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

Documentation should use:

    SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0

Canonical Texts
---------------

Local copies of referenced license texts are stored in:

    LICENSES/AGPL-3.0-or-later.txt
    LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt
    LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt

File-level SPDX identifiers and path-level notices control when they are
more specific than this overview.

No Trademark Grant
------------------

Software and documentation licenses do not grant trademark, branding,
endorsement, sponsorship, or official-project identity rights.

Non-Legal-Advice Boundary
-------------------------

This file records the project licensing posture and is not legal advice.
Formal relicensing, commercial distribution, patent strategy, trademark
registration, or release review should receive qualified legal review.
